### How ESP Microcontrollers Talk to Networks 🌐

One of the most exciting moments in engineering is seeing your microcontroller obtain an IP address and connect to your home router! Here is what real IoT code looks like:

```cpp
#include <WiFi.h> // ESP32 Wi-Fi library

const char* ssid = "MonsterLab_Network";
const char* password = "SecretBotPass123";

void setup() {
  Serial.begin(115200); // High-speed baud rate for ESP32!

  Serial.println("Booting ESP32 Wireless Transceiver...");
  WiFi.begin(ssid, password);

  while (WiFi.status() != WL_CONNECTED) {
    delay(500);
    Serial.print(".");
  }

  Serial.println("\nWi-Fi Connected!");
  Serial.print("Robot IP Address: ");
  Serial.println(WiFi.localIP());
}

void loop() {
  // Telemetry stream
}
```

#### Why This Matters for Monster Lab:
Once connected, your monster can send alerts to a phone, stream sensor readings to a web dashboard, or listen for commands from a web app anywhere in the world!
